-- A driving example for DESIGN.md's "Tier 3, not v0": `push` writes
-- `m[head]`, `pop` reads `m[tail]` -- two DIFFERENT registers, neither
-- one a scaled/offset function of the other (unlike v1/v2's same-base
-- affine case), and NEITHER individually bounded to a sub-range of the
-- other's (unlike `mem_disjoint_ranges.tr`'s `v6` proof): `head` and
-- `tail` each independently range over the FULL `[0, 8)` address space,
-- so no static partition of that space could ever separate them.
--
-- `push_count`/`pop_count` (each wrapping mod 16, the standard "extra
-- bit" FIFO pointer-counter technique -- capacity 8 < the counters' own
-- modulus 16, so `push_count - pop_count` unambiguously reads back the
-- true occupancy) are split so `push`/`pop` each only ever WRITE their
-- own counter (`push` never writes `pop_count`, `pop` never writes
-- `push_count`) -- but each rule's own fullness/emptiness guard
-- necessarily READS the OTHER rule's counter too (`push`'s guard needs
-- BOTH counts to compute occupancy; so does `pop`'s), which is not an
-- artifact of this example's construction -- it's inherent to any
-- correct FIFO: the producer genuinely cannot know "is there room"
-- without observing consumer progress, and vice versa. Confirmed
-- empirically, not assumed: `--explain-schedule` on this file reports
-- `rule push conflicts with rule pop: write meets read on {m,
-- push_count, pop_count}`, THREE shared facts, not just `m` -- and all
-- three are manifestations of the SAME underlying relational invariant
-- (see below), not independent confounds to be engineered away.
--
-- The actual reason `push`/`pop` never touch the same mem address
-- (while the buffer is neither empty nor full) is a RELATIONAL
-- invariant across the whole quadruple `(head, tail, push_count,
-- pop_count)` -- how far `head` has advanced past `tail` (mod 8) is
-- exactly `push_count - pop_count` (mod 16) -- not a fact about `head`
-- or `tail`'s own value in isolation. Every disjointness argument
-- `schedule.rs`/`bounds.rs` can make today (shared base, shared
-- power-of-two multiplier, one proven per-register interval, two
-- non-overlapping proven intervals) is a claim about ONE OR TWO
-- registers' own possible values; none of them can express "these two
-- registers' values are related by the history of writes to two OTHER
-- registers." This falls back to a real derived stall (`pop` only
-- fires when `push` is blocked or idle), exactly as conservative as if
-- `head`/`tail` had no `where` bound at all.
--
-- This is the genuinely general tier-3 shape DESIGN.md describes --
-- unlike `mem_disjoint_ranges.tr`'s note that "`m[i]` vs `m[j]` for two
-- arbitrary UNANNOTATED registers stays exactly as unprovable," this
-- case can't be closed by annotating `head`/`tail` with a TIGHTER
-- `where` bound either, since their full, correct range really is
-- `[0, 8)` each -- the missing fact is relational, not a tighter
-- per-register interval.
--
-- RESOLVED (bounds.rs's own half, the `mem_disjoint`/`schedule.rs`
-- consumer half is a SEPARATE, still-open undertaking -- see DESIGN.md):
-- the two `invariant` declarations below are exactly the relational
-- facts described above, checked by induction across every write site
-- in `push`/`pop` (including both firing the SAME cycle) the way
-- `bounds.rs` already checks an ordinary scalar `where` bound:
--
--   invariant push_count - pop_count < 9
--
-- ("occupancy" never exceeds capacity 8, so `push_count - pop_count`,
-- computed mod 16, is exactly the true occupancy in `[0, 8]`), and
--
--   invariant (head - tail - push_count + pop_count) % 8 = 0
--
-- (the real FIFO invariant: `head` has advanced exactly `push_count -
-- pop_count` positions past `tail`, mod 8). Both verify cleanly; the
-- first's inductive step genuinely needs `push`'s and `pop`'s own
-- guards as a hypothesis (proven via a NEW argument recognizing a
-- guard's own linear form, not just a bare `<def> < <const>` shape);
-- the second's deltas cancel to zero on every write site regardless of
-- either guard. `--explain-schedule` on this file is UNCHANGED by this
-- (still reports the same three-way conflict) -- proving these two
-- facts doesn't, by itself, retire the `push_count`/`pop_count` write-
-- meets-read hazard (an ordinary scalar hazard, unrelated to mem index
-- reasoning at all: each rule's guard reads the OTHER rule's counter).
-- The `schedule.rs`-side consumer that would use these facts to prove
-- `m[head]` vs `m[tail]` disjoint (and which, even then, would only
-- drop `m` from the reported conflict set, not clear the stall) is
-- deferred -- see DESIGN.md's "Tier 3" section for the full accounting
-- of why that consumer's payoff on THIS example is much smaller than
-- the bounds.rs half's own soundness argument.
module CircularBufferDisjoint {
    mem m : [8][8]

    reg head : [4] where head < 8 = 0
    reg tail : [4] where tail < 8 = 0
    reg push_count : [4] = 0
    reg pop_count : [4] = 0

    invariant push_count - pop_count < 9
    invariant (head - tail - push_count + pop_count) % 8 = 0

    in push_en : [1]
    in push_data : [8]
    in pop_en : [1]
    out pop_data : [8] = 0

    rule push {
        (push_en = 1)?
        (push_count - pop_count < 8)?
        m[head] := push_data
        if head < 7 {
            head := head + 1
        } else {
            head := 0
        }
        push_count := push_count + 1
    }

    rule pop {
        (pop_en = 1)?
        (push_count <> pop_count)?
        pop_data := m[tail]
        if tail < 7 {
            tail := tail + 1
        } else {
            tail := 0
        }
        pop_count := pop_count + 1
    }
}
